AB  - Abstract Nonlinear piezoelectric energy harvesters utilize actively designed structural dynamics to introduce nonlinear restoring forces, which enable large-amplitude oscillations over a low-frequency broadband range. This approach can enhance the efficiency of ambient vibrational energy harvesting under specific excitation conditions, compared to traditional linear systems. In this study, a novel percussive piezoelectric vibration energy harvester is developed, which, due to its symmetric cantilever beam structure, generates a knocking force under external excitation causing the harvester to switch between two states. The electromechanical coupling model of the percussive piezoelectric vibration energy harvester is established. Numerical simulations are performed to analyze the effects of different design parameters on its output performance. Three configurations of harvester prototypes are fabricated and tested. When the excitation frequency is 12 Hz and the axial compression distance of the PVDF beam is 0.2 mm, the prototype 1 achieves the maximum RMS output voltage of 3.61 V. When the excitation frequency is 12 Hz, the axial compression distance of the PVDF beam is 0.2 mm, and the load resistance is 0.2 MΩ, the maximum output power of prototype 1 reaches 32.7 μW. The validity of the proposed model is validated by comparing simulation results with experimental data. In addition, the experimental results of the three prototypes show that the structural parameters of the harvester can be effectively designed based on the vibration characteristics of the environmental vibration source.
